From c6389bb01f569ec027ae5dbda20d435ddfb624dc Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 00:52:49 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] VideoPlayer: Matroska editions: saved positions past a
 refused cut, the question, the read

Found by an independent review of the editions of a Matroska file.

- A position saved with an edition is placed back with the cuts its
  session really had. That session read the edit decision list files
  first and then added the edition's cuts through CEdl::AddEdit(),
  which refuses one that overlaps an edit already there (a cut, a mute,
  a commercial break). FileTimeOfSaved() added every cut of the edition
  back, so a position saved after a refused cut was placed too late, up
  to past the end of the file. Now each cut goes through AddEdit()'s
  own rule (EditAccepted(): start <= t < end, and an edit that
  surrounds another), the edits that cut nothing count as there, and
  the result lies within the file (0 to its duration, where known).
- Kodi reuses an item (resume, a playlist), and with it the edition
  chosen for an earlier playback. Where nothing is chosen this time
  (the question cancelled, the setting off, a file that is not read),
  the item now plays without it, as the file is stored
  (WithoutEdition()); a restart of the player keeps its edition.
- The question is asked before the messages of the playback before it
  are taken out of the queue (CApplication::PlayFile()): the loop of
  the modal dialog would otherwise handle them meanwhile.
- The header is read on a thread of its own while the application
  thread waits with the busy dialog (CGUIDialogBusy::WaitOnEvent(),
  cancellable): an open or a read of the network that does not return
  no longer freezes the interface. At the 10 s deadline, or when
  cancelled, the reading is abandoned (its next read returns
  AVERROR_EXIT, FFmpeg's interrupt answers) and the file plays as it is
  stored. Only a file on this device or on the local network
  (URIUtils::IsLocalOrLAN()) is read before playback: a file over the
  internet (an add-on's stream) would be opened twice, with range
  requests to its end, which servers that limit requests count.
  kodi.log says why a file was not read.
- The edition state of a bookmark (PLAYER_SET_STATE) belongs to the
  seek that follows it: every seek taken from the queue takes it, also
  one that is left out (a relative seek, another seek after it), so it
  never reaches a later seek.
- With the setting off nothing changes now. A position saved while no
  edition plays carries no state, as Kodi saves it; the earlier commits
  saved <hadesmkvedition>none</hadesmkvedition>, which still reads as
  the file as it is stored. For a file with editions to choose from, a
  position without a state is the file as it is stored, so a position
  saved by Kodi, or before editions could be chosen, is placed right in
  a session that plays an edition. This makes true "With the setting
  off nothing changes but those log lines" of "VideoPlayer: ask which
  edition of a Matroska file to play, and play an ordered one with
  cuts", which the commit after it ("VideoPlayer: the chapters of the
  chosen edition, and positions saved with their edition") no longer
  kept.

Limits: a reading that is abandoned while its server does not answer
keeps its thread until the server gives up. Not run on a device.

The host test replays six overlaps of an edit decision list file with
an edition against Kodi's own arithmetic, written out (71 checks; nine
mutations make it fail); mkveditions_test, 110 checks.
